# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  grammar_l3.la — ITEM 5, L3: THE GRAMMAR PARSING ITSELF.
#
#  L1 made the grammar first-class DATA; L2 gave GPARSE, an interpreter over
#  that data, differentially checked against parser.la (60/60, both sides, via
#  fuzz_grammar.py). L3 is the self-application: **GPARSE, driven by the data
#  grammar, parses the source file that DEFINES the data grammar.**
#
#  ── HOW IT COMPOSES, AND WHY IT IS A CONCATENATION ──────────────────
#  Run as:  cat grammar_l3.la grammar.la <parser.la minus its MAIN>
#  · `import("parser.la")` does NOT work: parser.la carries no `export` line, so
#    the import binds nothing. Concatenation is this codebase's existing answer
#    (build.sh does exactly this for monosemy_test: `cat canon.la monosemy_test.la`).
#  · ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. Redefinition takes the FIRST binding, and parser.la
#    and grammar.la use INCOMPATIBLE list encodings —
#        parser.la : NIL = PAIR(FALSE)(""), CONS = tagged pair
#        grammar.la: NIL = la n. la c. n,   CONS = Scott
#    GPARSE deconstructs its token list Scott-style, so grammar.la's must win;
#    hence grammar.la precedes parser.la. This is safe because parser.la's LEXER
#    (SKIP_SPACES/LEX_ONE/READ_IDENT/READ_STRING/MKTOK/NEXT_TOKEN) uses PAIR and
#    strings ONLY — verified: zero NIL/CONS references in that region. Concatenating
#    them in the other order fails loudly, which is how the incompatibility was found.
#  · parser.la's own MAIN is trimmed; otherwise IT wins (first binding) and parses
#    kernel.la instead of running this gate.
#
#  ── THE BOUND THIS RESPECTS ─────────────────────────────────────────
#  ★ THE LEXER IS NOT RE-IMPLEMENTED. L2's stated bound is that GPARSE consumes
#    TOKEN TYPES and lexing is parser.la's job. So TOKENIZE below is a thin driver
#    over parser.la's OWN NEXT_TOKEN, keeping the type names it produces. A lexer
#    written here for this test could be tuned until the self-parse passed, which
#    is exactly the result this must not be.
#
#  ── WHAT IS AND IS NOT CLAIMED ──────────────────────────────────────
#  ★ CLAIMED: the data grammar accepts the full 144-line source of the module that
#    defines it, and REJECTS a corrupted version of that same token stream.
#  ★ NOT CLAIMED: that GPARSE is parser.la. L2's associativity blind spot is
#    unchanged — app_tail is left-associative and a verdict-only differential
#    cannot see associativity. L3 inherits that bound and does not repair it.
#  ★ NOT CLAIMED: unbounded self-parse. This is ONE file — its own. That is the
#    file the discriminator names, and the honest word for it is BOUNDED.
#
#  ── ★★ THE MEASURED BOUND, AND IT IS A PERFORMANCE BOUND, NOT A GRAMMAR ONE ──
#  grammar.la lexes to 1689 tokens. TOKENIZE handles that quickly — the lexer is
#  not the cost. GPARSE is: MATCH does NAIVE BACKTRACKING alternation, re-scanning
#  from the same position on every failed arm, and P_MODULE is a STAR over an ALT,
#  so the work grows superlinearly in token count. Measured on prefixes cut at
#  definition boundaries (so a slice never ends mid-definition, which would confuse
#  "rejected because truncated" with "rejected because broken"):
#
#      defs  lines  tokens   accept  reject-corrupted   time
#        4     47      84      T           F             2s
#        8     51     145      T           F             5s
#       12     58     209      T           F             7s
#       16     62     333      T           F            15s
#       20     78     538      T           F            37s
#
#       ALL   144    1689      T           F          1637s (27 min)
#
#  ★★ THE WHOLE FILE SELF-PARSES. Witnessed 2026-08-26 19:05: accept=T, reject=F on
#    the complete 1689-token stream, in 27 minutes. An earlier version of this header
#    said the full run "has not been witnessed to complete" — that was TRUE WHEN
#    WRITTEN and is now FALSE; it is corrected rather than quietly dropped, because a
#    stale bound reads as a real one.
#  ★ EVERY size self-parses CORRECTLY — accept=T and reject=F throughout, so the
#    grammar was never what was in doubt; the interpreter is slow. Cost grows about
#    n^3.3 (538 tok/37s -> 1689 tok/1637s is 3.1x the tokens for 44x the time),
#    which is the signature of MATCH re-scanning from the same position on every
#    failed arm of a STAR-over-ALT.
#  ★ THE REJECT ARM IS WHAT MAKES THE ACCEPTS MEAN ANYTHING. Without it a GPARSE
#    that returned TRUE unconditionally would produce an identical accept column.
#  ★ WHY THE GATE STILL USES A PREFIX: build time, not evidence. 27 minutes is ~15%
#    on a 3-hour build for a fact already established out of band. Point SELF_SRC at
#    grammar.la directly to reproduce the whole-file result.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# TOKENIZE — parser.la's own lexer, driven to exhaustion, keeping TYPES only.
# Builds with grammar.la's Scott CONS/NIL (forward-referenced), which is what
# GPARSE consumes.
glyph TOKENIZE = Z(la self. la s.
    (la r. (la tk.
        IF(str_eq(TOK_TYPE(tk))("eof"))(la _. NIL)
          (la _. CONS(TOK_TYPE(tk))(self(SND(r)))))(FST(r)))(NEXT_TOKEN(s)))

glyph TLEN = Z(la self. la l. l(0)(la h. la t. add(1)(self(t))))

# THE SUBJECT: this module's own grammar definition, read from disk.
# ★ THE SUBJECT IS SUPPLIED AS A FILE so the same module can be run against the
#   WHOLE of grammar.la (the true self-parse, which is slow — see the bound above)
#   and against a definition-boundary PREFIX of it (which is what build.sh gates,
#   because a gate may only assert what has been WITNESSED to terminate).
#   In both cases the subject is grammar.la's own source: the prefix is a prefix
#   OF THE SELF, not a different corpus. build.sh writes l3_subject.la.
glyph SELF_SRC    = read_file("l3_subject.la")
glyph SELF_TOKENS = TOKENIZE(SELF_SRC)

# ★ THE SELF-PARSE.
glyph L3_ACCEPT = GPARSE("module")(SELF_TOKENS)

# ★ THE REJECT ARM. A self-parse that can only say T is not a gate — it would pass
#   identically if GPARSE returned TRUE unconditionally. Prefixing an `eq` token
#   makes the stream start with `=`, which no production can begin, so a working
#   GPARSE must refuse it. Same tokens otherwise, so nothing but validity differs.
glyph L3_REJECT = GPARSE("module")(CONS("eq")(SELF_TOKENS))

glyph L3 = concat(BSTR(L3_ACCEPT))(BSTR(L3_REJECT))

glyph SEQ3 = la a. la b. b
glyph LN1 = print(concat("ITEM5 L3 self-parse: tokens=")(int_to_str(TLEN(SELF_TOKENS))))
glyph LN2 = print(concat("L3 accept(own source)=")(concat(BSTR(L3_ACCEPT))(concat("  reject(corrupted)=")(BSTR(L3_REJECT)))))
glyph LN3 = print(concat("L3 verdict [want TF] = ")(L3))
glyph LN4 = print("(the data grammar parses the source that defines it; BOUNDED to that one file)")
glyph MAIN = SEQ3(LN1)(SEQ3(LN2)(SEQ3(LN3)(LN4)))
